Answer: An interface is declared with the interface keyword instead of the class keyword. It gives pure abstraction and also standardization. The class that uses it writes implements and is called the implementing class.

```java
interface Calculator {
void add(int a, int b);
}
```

Answer: Suppose Calculator1, Calculator2 and Calculator3 all perform addition, but each class uses a different method name. A developer has to remember the method name of every class, and with 300 classes it is impossible to remember all of them.

```java
class Calculator1 { void add(int a, int b) { } }
class Calculator2 { void sum(int a, int b) { } }
class Calculator3 { void addition(int a, int b) { } }
```

